<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ===================================================================================
         V1 BASELINE (SCRUM-107, owner directive 2026-08-08). Spring Batch 6.0.4 metadata for
         MRR, table-prefixed MRR_BATCH_ so this service is the single writer of its own job
         repository (R-34). EXIT_MESSAGE is widened to TEXT so a CockroachDB driver cause
         chain is never truncated.

         PURE XML, TYPED TAGS, ONE CHANGESET PER OBJECT. This replaces the sqlFile that used
         to carry the autogenerated Spring Batch DDL, and the replacement is what removes all
         three pieces of retrofit scar tissue at once rather than working around them:

           - <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum> is GONE. It existed because editing the .sql
             changed the single changeset's checksum on databases that had already run it.
             There are no such databases in v1, and there is no longer a single changeset.
           - IF NOT EXISTS on every CREATE is GONE. It existed because one changeset covering
             nine objects is all-or-nothing: a pod SIGKILLed mid-file left the tables present,
             the sequences absent and no history row, so the re-run either collided with an
             existing table or skipped the whole file and left the schema permanently broken.
             Nine changesets fix that properly. Each object commits its own history row, so a
             kill resumes at the exact object it died on, which is what the defensive clause
             was approximating.
           - The raw .sql file is GONE, so the changelog is portable typed XML throughout
             (rules/be/java/persistence.md, pure-XML mandate).

         EVERY changeset here carries an onFail="MARK_RAN" guard on the object it creates.
         The earlier claim that none was needed confused two different statements: MRR is
         indeed the only service that creates MRR_BATCH_ objects, which is not the same as
         those objects being absent. Measured on the stage cluster 2026-09-11, dcre_man
         already held all six batch tables and all three batch sequences for ALL TEN
         mandates services from the pre-v1 mechanism, while each service's changelog table
         listed only the retired sqlFile-era ids and none of the ids below. That is the
         state in which Liquibase issues CREATE TABLE against an existing relation and the
         pod exits 78, observed live on CRW. No mandates pod has been seen reaching this
         changeset: MRG, the only mandates service currently deployed to stage, fails
         earlier on a checksum mismatch in 003-man-reporting.xml and 008-man-status-
         history.xml, which this changelog does not touch. One object per changeset is what
         makes each guard exact: every precondition names precisely what its changeset
         creates, so it can never mark unfinished work as applied.

         Constraint names (JOB_INST_UN, JOB_INST_EXEC_FK, ...) are Spring Batch's own and are
         deliberately NOT service-prefixed, matching the DDL this replaces. CockroachDB scopes
         index and constraint names to the table, so the ten services' copies coexist in the
         one dcre_man exactly as they did before.
         =================================================================================== -->

    <changeSet id="mrr-002-batch-job-instance" author="dcre">
        <!-- A-82: this changeset gained a re-run guard AFTER it had already been applied,
             which changes the enclosing checksum without changing one byte of the DDL
             below. ANY rather than a pinned value: kind-dcre-dev stored 9:d21030910c4fe453fd687a01243f98ca,
             and every environment that migrated at a different time holds a different
             one, so a pin is correct on exactly one database and silently wrong on the
             rest. The preConditions block is what keeps the re-run safe; the checksum
             is not load-bearing here. Same call, same reason, as prr 002-batch-metadata. -->
        <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum>
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="MRR_BATCH_JOB_INSTANCE"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <createTable tableName="MRR_BATCH_JOB_INSTANCE">
            <column name="JOB_INSTANCE_ID" type="BIGINT">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="VERSION" type="BIGINT"/>
            <column name="JOB_NAME" type="VARCHAR(100)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="JOB_KEY" type="VARCHAR(32)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="MRR_BATCH_JOB_INSTANCE" columnNames="JOB_NAME, JOB_KEY"
                             constraintName="JOB_INST_UN"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="mrr-002-batch-job-execution" author="dcre">
        <!-- A-82: this changeset gained a re-run guard AFTER it had already been applied,
             which changes the enclosing checksum without changing one byte of the DDL
